src('SRC023','Anomalous Echoes Captured by a B-52 Airborne Radarscope Camera: A Preliminary Report','Martin Shough','Sign Oral History Project (minotb52ufo.com)','2005-2011','Independent radar analysis (PDF, ~36,000 words)','SECONDARY','https://minotb52ufo.com/shough/pdf/SHOUGH_Report.pdf','','frame descriptions; sections 3-7','%s'%A,'INC052;TEC008;CLM083;EVD023;EVD024','Frame-by-frame description of scans 771-783; quotes Werlich memo ("2½ mile in 3 sec or about 3,000 mi/hr"); reconstructs the "3900" figure as the average of two paint-time-corrected rates (~1,990 kt and ~5,870 kt co-altitude, relative to B-52; ~3,900 kt); notes 3000 mph cannot be recovered from photos; only ~1/10 of original frames survive; tests meteor, AP, multiple-trip, ECM, balloon, aircraft hypotheses; concludes none fully convincing.','Independent of Tulien/Poher in method but part of the same project; "preliminary"; assumptions (co-altitude, single target, range-scale 5 NM inferred as no scale indicator on photos) stated.','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED','EXAMINED')
src('SRC024','Analysis of Radar and Air-Visual UFO Observations on 24 October 1968 at Minot AFB','Claude Poher','Sign Oral History Project (minotb52ufo.com)','2005-08 (amended 2006)','Technical analysis (PDF, 62 MB)','SECONDARY','https://minotb52ufo.com/poher/pdf/POHER_Report.pdf','','Part 1; Appendix 1','%s'%A,'INC052;EVD025;EVD026','Estimates 189 g / Mach 8 for the 6,100->1,930 m move in <3 s; reconstructs B-52 trajectory timing: radarscope clock runs ~9 min ahead of approach-controller clock; photos 771-783 coincide with radio loss at ~0858 COM, ~21 nm from Deering TACAN (i.e., early in the event) - conflicts with Shough.','Poher states the UFO hypothesis ("exceptional UFO case"); only abstract, Part 1 and Appendix 1 portions read by me.','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED','EXAMINED')
src('SRC025','Project Blue Book Final Case Report, Minot AFB 24 Oct 1968 (FTD 13 Nov 1968 + Discussion of Background Information)','Lt Col Hector Quintanilla Jr. (FTD)','USAF Project Blue Book (reproduction on minotb52ufo.com)','1968-11-13','Official evaluation (PDF, 23 pp)','PRIMARY','https://minotb52ufo.com/pdf/Blue-Book-Final-Case-Report.pdf','Blue Book case 12,548','pp. 1-4 text + atch','%s'%A,'GOV015;INC052;EXP024;EXP026','Confirms: Sirius + B-52; plasma similar to ball lightning for radar contact/UHF loss; Vega or ground light/plasma for air visual; "physical violation of the lock of OSCAR 7 does not seem to be related to UFOs"; says B-52 arrived on a 50-nm radius clearance flying "vertical S" upper airwork "at almost the same time as the first sighting"; later sightings "correlate with the B-52 making a VOR penetration, low approach, and missed approach"; temperature inversion and haze; no data submitted on 0335 local visual / 0352 weather-radar paint.','Official explanation itself criticised by Shough/Tulien/Hastings; scan quality good; reproduction hosted by a case investigator.','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED','EXAMINED')
src('SRC026','Base Operations Dispatcher Log, Minot AFB, 24 Oct 1968 (Zulu times)','Base Operations dispatcher','USAF Minot AFB (Blue Book file doc 16)','1968-10-24','Contemporaneous log (PDF extract)','PRIMARY','https://minotb52ufo.com/pdf/Base-Operations-Dispatcher-Log.pdf','Blue Book case 12,548 doc 16','','%s'%A,'INC052;GOV016;EVD027','Entries 0800Z-1010Z: bright object S/E of N-7 hovering, 0828-0830Z "jet engines heard"; 0926Z B-52 "had on radar 20,000 feet" object followed B-52 15 mi, radio lost; 0940Z "B-52 landed"; 0940-1010Z object 3 mi west of N-7, stationary/on ground, orange->white->green, later "approximately 50 feet off ground".','Only extract of the log; operator wording informal; time base Zulu per 0800Z=0300 CDT.','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED','EXAMINED')
src('SRC027','B-52 radarscope photographs 771-784 (14 frames), scans of Blue Book prints','USAF 5th Bomb Wing (Clark prints)','Blue Book file 12,548 (reproduction on minotb52ufo.com)','1968-10-24','Photographic scans (PDF)','PRIMARY','https://minotb52ufo.com/pdf/All-Radarscope-Photographs.pdf','Blue Book doc 32','14 pages','%s'%A,'TEC008;INC052','Frame 771 viewed at 70 dpi: half-completed sweep, three inner range rings, bezel azimuth ring, analogue clock, data plate, numbering meter "771". I could not independently locate the faint echo at that resolution.','Prints, not original negatives; no range-scale indicator on frame.','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED','EXAMINED')
ev('EVD023',"Werlich's Memo for the Record (24 Oct 1968), quoted by Shough: 'Initially the target traveled approximately 2½ mile in 3 sec or at about 3,000 mi/hr' (arithmetic: 2.5 mi/3 s = 3,000 mph). His 29 Oct telex instead says an echo closed from 3 mi to ~1 mi off the left wing, with no speed. The Blue Book file therefore has TWO different official speed statements; neither is '2 miles in 3 s = 3,900 mph'.",'DOCUMENT_QUOTATION','SRC023 (quoting Blue Book file); verification of original memo pending','INC052;CLM083','12862','HIGH','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD024',"Origin of '3,900': Shough reconstructs it as the simple average of two rates computed from echo displacements relative to the B-52 between frames 771-772 and 772-773, using antenna paint-time-corrected intervals (3.8 s and 1.3 s): ~1,990 kt and ~5,870 kt, co-altitude upper limits, mean ~3,900 KNOTS (~4,450 statute mph). Hence Clark's number is knots, a mean of very unequal rates, an upper limit, and not '2 miles per sweep'.",'ANALYSIS_RESULT','SRC023 section 6','INC052;CLM083;TEC008','12862','HIGH','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD025',"Analyst recomputation (this project): from Shough's measured echo positions (771: 138 deg/1.62 NM; 772: 242 deg/1.05 NM; 773: 40 deg/1.05 NM; azimuth from nose, slant range) the displacements are 2.13 and 2.06 NM. With a naive 3.0 s per frame: ~2,560 and ~2,470 kt (~2,950/2,850 mph) relative speed. With Shough's paint-time intervals (3.8, 1.3 s): ~2,020 kt and ~5,710 kt (matches Shough ~1,990/5,870 within ~3%); their simple mean is ~3,865 kt (reproducing '3,900'), but the time-weighted mean over the 5.1 s is only ~2,960 kt (~3,400 mph). Script: database/build/analysis/minot_speed.py.",'ANALYST_CALCULATION','Derived from SRC023 numerical values; not independent measurement of the photographs','INC052;CLM083','12862','HIGH','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD026',"Assumptions behind any speed figure: (i) the three echoes are one target (Shough: cannot be justified from two possibly unrelated echoes; elongated echoes are suggestive but not conclusive); (ii) co-altitude (a depression angle lowers true separation; e.g. at 50 deg the naive horizontal speed would be ~1,650 kt, ~1,900 mph); (iii) range scale inferred (~5 NM display, no indicator on photographs); (iv) only 14 of probably >100 frames survive. The result is an upper-limit estimate, not a measurement of a 3,900 mph object.",'METHODOLOGICAL_LIMIT','SRC023; analyst calculation','INC052;TEC008','12862','HIGH','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD027',"Timeline dispute between analysts: Shough concludes frames 771-783 were taken late in the event near the missed approach (so the 'fast' echo transit is not the start); Poher concludes the radarscope clock (0906Z) runs ~9 min ahead of the approach-controller clock so frames coincide with radio loss at ~0858 COM when the B-52 was ~21 nm out (early). The question of when the high-speed transit occurred relative to ground sightings is unresolved in the sources I read.",'ANALYST_DISAGREEMENT','SRC023 vs SRC024 Appendix 1','INC052','12862','HIGH','DISPUTED')
ev('EVD028',"Dispatcher log vs B-52 explanation: entries 0828-0830Z mention jet engines; the B-52 low approach/missed approach was ~0904-0917 COM (Poher). So the Blue Book statement that 'later sightings ... correlate with' the B-52 low approach is plausible for entries after ~0900Z but not demonstrated for the 0800-0858Z entries; and entries 0940-1010Z (after the log says the B-52 landed at 0940Z) describe a stationary object west of N-7 on/near the ground, which the B-52 cannot explain.",'DOCUMENT_COMPARISON','SRC025; SRC026; SRC024','INC052;EXP024;EXP026','12811','HIGH','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD029',"Analyst astronomical check (this project; simple GMST/precession, Minot AFB 48.42N 101.36W, 24 Oct 1968): Sirius altitude 11-23 deg, azimuth 132-162 deg (SE to SSE) from 0800Z to 1010Z; Vega 7 deg->-2 deg altitude, azimuth 325-348 deg (NW-NNW), setting ~0940Z. Dispatcher entries 0800Z 'S/E of N-7' fit Sirius direction; entries 0940-1010Z 'west of N-7' do not (~110 deg off); Blue Book cites Vega at azimuth 345 at 0435 local (matches my 342 deg) but the pilot's light was 'west-northwest' (~50 deg away) - requires check of the AF Forms 117. Script: database/build/analysis/minot_sirius.py.",'ANALYST_CALCULATION','Computed; positions accurate to ~0.5 deg; atmospheric refraction ignored','INC052;EXP024;EXP026','12916','HIGH','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD030',"Blue Book's own text (SRC025) says no data were submitted for the possible 0335 local air visual and 0352 weather-radar paint, and that the pilot's Form 117 puts his sighting at ~0435 local, not 0335: official file acknowledges data gaps in the timeline; Hastings' narrative does not.",'DOCUMENT_QUOTATION','SRC025 p.3','INC052','12816','MEDIUM','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
clm('CLM087',"Werlich's initial memo gave the UFO's initial speed as ~3,000 mph (2½ miles in 3 seconds).","Werlich memo via Shough",'DOCUMENT','MEDIUM',12862,'PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
clm('CLM088',"Hastings' statement that Clark derived 3,900 mph from two miles in one 3-second sweep is not supported: arithmetic gives 2,400 mph; Shough reconstructs 3,900 KNOTS as an average of two paint-time-corrected co-altitude upper-limit rates.","Analyst finding (EVD024, EVD025)",'ANALYST_FINDING','HIGH',12862,'PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
